{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor consistently recognized the high-risk neonatal infection context, escalated promptly when low temperature and poor feeding appeared, and repeatedly converted uncertainty about records, labs, follow-up, and vitamin AD into executable caregiver actions. All responsibility-chain duties were met, with clear red flags, infection-workup follow-up, medication boundaries, document handling, and caregiver receipt. Multiple high-order points were completed with meaningful impact, especially correcting false reassurance from no fever/negative interim results, repairing follow-up responsibility, correcting vitamin AD misunderstanding, and dynamically reprioritizing to urgent in-person evaluation. The residual follow-up appointment detail was left as an open action but had already been made safe and executable, so the trajectory meets Perfect rather than merely Strong.",
  "closure_assessment": {
    "citations": [
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_02093",
        "explanation": "The doctor made the remaining follow-up action explicit before discharge, including whether the appointment was already made or must be arranged.",
        "quote": "复诊时间具体是哪一天？已经约好了还是我们自己挂号？"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_02093",
        "explanation": "The doctor gave an immediate emergency fallback that remains safe while the appointment detail is finalized.",
        "quote": "有一条就要立刻去急诊"
      }
    ],
    "executable": true,
    "explanation": "The episode ends with a safe open transition: the family has clear red flags, medication boundaries, and the next action to confirm exact 1–3 day neonatal/ pediatric follow-up and possible CBC/CRP review before leaving. Although the final family message still reports that the exact appointment was not fully fixed, the doctor had already given the precise action to resolve that gap and not defer it until after discharge.",
